00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:34.200
So based on your trading performance, the
Kelly Criterion just basically tells you
how much you should be committing in
capital terms to your next trade.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.200
It started in the 1950s on the horse
racing track.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.200
Gamblers used to use it when they went to
the horses, to the racetrack.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:55.200
So they go to the racetrack with $100 and
they go on race one.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.200
Well, let's put the question out there.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:05.200
If you go to the racetrack with $100 and
there's 10 races today, how much do you
put on race number one?

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:10.200
Okay, so you lose your $10, how much do
you put on race number two?

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.200
Yeah, so what you're doing is you're
taking capital away from yourself for each
trade, yeah?

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.200
In the reverse, walk onto the racetrack
with $100, you put $10 on and your horse
comes in.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.200
And you make a total of $18.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.200
What would you do next?

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:21.200
Yeah, what would you do next?

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.200
Yeah, you take more risk, yeah?

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.200
So the Kelly Criterion basically, what
would you do next?

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.200
How much do you put on race number three?

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.200
Yeah, so what you're doing is you're
taking capital away from yourself for each
trade, yeah?

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.200
In the reverse, walk onto the racetrack
with $100, you put $10 on and your horse
comes in.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.200
And you make a total of $18.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.200
What would you do next?

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.200
Yeah, you take more risk, yeah?

00:07:44.200 --> 00:08:03.200
So the Kelly Criterion basically rewards
traders who make money by saying you put
more capital on your next trade and takes
capital away from traders who always lose.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.200
The marginal rate that you take off and
put on.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.200
That's what Kelly tells you to do, yeah?

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:17.200
But it's a great litmus test when you look
