00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:17.200
Now, if you're a good trader and you're
winning, you should be pulling from box A.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.200
You should be taking more risk.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:20.200
How do you take more risk?

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:21.200
You do it twofold.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:29.200
You either increase exposure in your
trading account and or you add more money
to your trading account further down the
line.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:41.200
So for example, if you open the trading
account with $25,000 deposited on margin
and your exposure was $100,000 to $125,000
to begin with.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:43.200
So four to five times.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:54.200
And over, let's say, an eight month
period, you were pretty consistent and you
managed to double your $25,000 to $50,000.

00:22:55.200 --> 00:23:05.200
So that would mean, based on your exposure
levels, you're getting a 20% to 25% return
on your money and doing this net of
commission on a consistent basis.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.200
Let's say you've done this over an eight
to 12 month period.

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:11.200
Well, what's happening here?

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:22.200
You're probably being told at this time by
the Kelly Criterion that you're pretty
good at trading and also your profit is
telling you that you're pretty good at
trading.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.200
So you should be pulling from box A here.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:32.200
You should be increasing your exposure
levels and you should be thinking about
putting more money in your trading
account.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.200
Now, what do we do if you get the
opposite?

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:38.200
Let's say you're losing money
consistently.

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:48.200
Well, you're just going to hit one of your
portfolio construction parameters, which
is stop losses on your positions and stop
losses on your overall portfolio.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:54.200
And your stop losses are there so you can
actually preserve capital and try again.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:58.200
So you don't necessarily have to take
money out of your trading account.

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:04.200
It will just mean that overall you've been
bad at trading over that time period.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:19.200
And your W is going to be bad and your R
is going to be bad, which means a
combination of your winning probability
factor, your W and your risk management
capabilities, your R.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.200
A combination of those just means that
you're bad.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:29.200
So pulling from box A and pulling from box
B, that's what Kelly is going to show you.

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:37.200
And that's what you're going to do when
you start out properly as a professional
portfolio manager or taking this approach.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:48.200
Second thing that's important with the
Kelly criterion is the ability to identify
what your strengths and weaknesses are.

00:24:49.200 --> 00:25:00.200
So if your W is really good, so your ideas
are good, but your risk management is very
poor, then it's obvious that you need to
work on your risk management and you need
to increase R.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:03.200
You need to work on making that a better
number.

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:11.200
Also, if your risk management is very
good, but your ideas are poor, it's very
obvious that you need to spend more time.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:17.200
You need to put a bit more effort into
generating higher quality ideas.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:30.200
But also at the gatekeeping side, you
might want to try and time these trades
better or with your price action
indicators, make sure you're taking the
correct approach.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.200
So it can come in the whole idea
generation phase, but also with
gatekeeping.

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:39.200
So if your W is bad, that's what you need
to be focusing on.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:26:07.200
Now that we've been through the Kelly
criterion and the self-awareness statistic
that is very important, what you need to
understand as well is that with your
portfolio construction parameters, if the
Kelly criterion is recommending that you
do more on a single position, on a single
trade in your portfolio versus what your
own percentage limits are on your single
position assets.

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:14.200
You probably should not be going above
your own self-administered statistics.

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:21.200
So for example, the Kelly criterion is
recommending that your next trade or a
single position should be a maximum of 15
or 20%.

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:23.200
That's all fine.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:25.200
That's good to know.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:38.200
But if your limits are 10% in your own
portfolio, you should stick to them
because you don't want to be investing too
much money into one position or one idea.

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:47.200
Your own position limits stop you from
being overconfident and putting too much
money into one idea.

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:51.200
So the Kelly criterion, if it's
recommending more, that's great.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:53.200
Just keep it in the background.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:54.200
Keep the Kelly criterion running.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:59.200
You should absolutely overlay it with your
own risk management parameters.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.200
So we've been through all this stuff now.

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:04.200
We've been through having discipline one,
two, three and four.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:08.200
And doing that, we went through training
psychology.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:10.200
Make sure you understand that.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.200
Make sure you've got the foundations
correct.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:19.200
Try to deploy the box A and the box B
strategy into other areas of your life.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:21.200
This is the mode of thinking of a trader.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:24.200
And this is the mode of thinking that you
have to get used to.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:28.200
Secondly, we looked at portfolio
construction parameters.

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:33.200
Definitely deploy some or all of those
parameters into your portfolio.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:38.200
If you don't, you're just falling into the
trap of being an amateur trader.

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:40.200
And that's something you don't want.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:41.200
You need to protect your downside.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:45.200
You protect your downside because you
understand the psychology.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:48.200
And you understand your weaknesses as a
human.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:51.200
We also looked at the hedging techniques.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:53.200
We've been through beta.

00:27:54.200 --> 00:27:55.200
You can use the basic beta approach.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.200
You can also use the advanced approach.

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:01.200
If your objective is to work at an
investment bank or a hedge fund.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:05.200
And we've also been through the
self-awareness statistic of the Kelly
criterion.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:09.200
So you know at any one point in time how
good you are as a trader.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:11.200
And your own abilities as a trader.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.200
So you can deploy capital to yourself
responsibly.

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:19.200
So we've been through the whole of risk
management now.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:22.200
We're going to move on to the next video.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:35.200
Make sure you go through the risk
management and self-awareness statistic,
the Kelly criterion, over a number of
times before you actually start committing
capital to real trades and building a
portfolio.

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:40.200
It's very important stuff and will
separate good traders from bad traders.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:46.200
And you obviously want to be a good trader
that makes money consistently over time.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.200
If you don't do these things you simply
won't be.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:53.200
So please make sure you go through these
videos many times.

